{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>For when the people come to me to inquire of Elohim</b> Moses answered his father-in-law: they need to stand over me a long time each day, for many matters come before me — for when the people come to me to inquire of Elohim, to pray concerning their illnesses and to inform them of what will befall them. For this is called 'inquiring of Elohim,' and so they would do with the prophets, as it says (I Samuel 9:9), 'Previously in Israel, when a man went to inquire of Elohim, he would say: Come, let us go to the seer.' And likewise, 'And you shall inquire of HaShem from him, saying: Shall I recover from this illness?' (II Kings 8:8) — that he should pray for him and inform him whether his prayer has been heard. And likewise, 'And she went to inquire of HaShem' (Genesis 25:22), as I explained there. And furthermore, I judge them: <b>for when they have a matter, it comes to me and I judge</b>. And furthermore, I teach them Torah: <b>and I make known to them the statutes of Elohim and His laws</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:10:28.769708516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}